/* ============================================================================
   number-classification.epher -- deficient, perfect or abundant

   Classifies a number by its proper divisor sum ps(n): ps(n) < n makes n
   deficient, ps(n) = n makes it perfect, and ps(n) > n makes it abundant
   (the Greek classification of Nicomachus, c. 100 AD; Hardy and Wright,
   ch. 16, section 16.8). The script computes ps(n) with the classroom
   pair method, since epher has no divisor-sum builtin, and a def turns
   the verdict into a word. Checks: 8 is deficient (1 + 2 + 4 = 7), 12 is
   abundant (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 16), and 6 and 28 are perfect.

   Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
   ch. 16, section 16.8. Results are exact.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/divisors/number-classification.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\divisors\number-classification.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/divisors/number-classification.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// pairdiv(n, i) adds each divisor of n with its partner n / i (a square
// adds its root once), so pairdiv(n, 1) is sigma(n); ps subtracts n.
def pairdiv(n, i) = if i * i > n then 0 else if mod(n, i) == 0 then (if i * i == n then i + pairdiv(n, i + 1) else i + n / i + pairdiv(n, i + 1)) else pairdiv(n, i + 1)
def sigma(n) = pairdiv(n, 1)
def ps(n) = sigma(n) - n
def kind(n) = if ps(n) == n then "perfect" else if ps(n) > n then "abundant" else "deficient"

// ---- the verdicts -----------------------------------------------------------------------
for x in {6, 8, 12, 28, 30, 100} do kind(x)
print("8 is deficient:", ps(8) < 8)            // 1 + 2 + 4 = 7
print("12 is abundant:", ps(12) > 12)          // proper sum 16
print("30 is abundant:", ps(30) > 30)          // proper sum 42
print("proper sum of 100:", ps(100))           // 117: 100 is abundant
/* ---- expected output ----
= {perfect, deficient, abundant, perfect, abundant, abundant}
= 8 is deficient: true
= 12 is abundant: true
= 30 is abundant: true
= proper sum of 100: 117
*/
